**user**:
> How can I make a document where all of the changes I have made are highlighted? I have the original and new version as either a google doc or a Word document.
**assistant**:
> To create a document where all changes are highlighted, you can use the "Compare Documents" feature available in Word or explore third-party tools for Google Docs. Here’s a step-by-step guide for each option:
> 
> ### Microsoft Word
> 
> 1. **Open Microsoft Word.**
> 2. **Go to the "Review" tab** in the ribbon at the top.
> 3. **Click on "Compare"** in the "Compare" group.
> 4. Select **"Compare..."** from the dropdown menu.
> 5. In the dialog box, select your **original document** and **revised document** using the browse buttons.
> 6. [Optional] Configure settings under "More" to specify what types of changes to track and how to display them.
> 7. Click **"OK"** to compare the documents.
> 
> Word will generate a new document showing all changes, highlighting insertions, deletions, and formatting changes.
> 
> ### Google Docs
> 
> Google Docs does not have a built-in compare feature, but you can use a third-party tool or workaround:
> 
> 1. **Use a third-party tool like "Draftback" or "diffchecker.com"** which can compare two text files or documents.
> 2. **Download both documents as Word files** and follow the Word comparison process above, opening them in Word.
> 3. Alternatively, **manually copy each document's text** into a compare tool and observe the differences.
> 
> ### Manual Highlighting in Google Docs
> 
> If you prefer to stay within Google Docs:
> 
> 1. **Create a new Google Doc** and copy and paste the text from your original document.
> 2. **Use the "Version History" feature** to track changes in one document. (Access via "File" > "Version History" > "See version history").
> 3. Alternatively, manually highlight changes: 
>    - Copy any new or changed sections from your revised version and paste them into the original in a different color or highlight.
>    - Use additional formatting to indicate differences, such as strikethrough for deletions.
> 
> These methods should help you highlight changes effectively, depending on your tool preference. If you're collaborating, consider preserving a tracked version for transparency between contributors.
**user**:
> Okay, I'm doing this in Word. I got the compared document, and it shows everything as track changes. Can I change that to just be highlighted, or produce a PDF with the changes shown but without the column of "James formatted X", "James inserted Y".
**assistant**:
> Yes, you can simplify the display of changes in your compared document in Word and produce a PDF without the "James formatted X", "James inserted Y" annotations. Here’s how to do that:
> 
> ### Change Track Changes to Highlighted
> 
> 1. **Accept/Reject Changes**: First, you can choose to accept or reject changes. If you want to keep everything highlighted but without the annotations, follow these steps:
>    - Go to the **“Review” tab**.
>    - Use the **“Accept”** or **“Reject”** buttons to finalize changes. When you accept changes, it will remain formatted as you’ve set.
> 
> 2. **Adjust Track Changes Settings**:
>    - If you still want the tracked changes to be visible but simplified, go to **“Review” > “Track Changes” > “Change Tracking Options”**.
>    - Modify the options for **“Inserted Text”** and **“Deleted Text”**. You can set inserted text to be highlighted instead of shown with comments.
> 
> 3. **View as Final**: Another option is to set the document to "Final":
>    - On the **“Review” tab**, find **“Track Changes”** and click on **“Display for Review”**. Change it to **“Final”**.
>    - This will show you the finished document with all changes accepted, and you can decide how to handle formatting options.
> 
> ### Save as PDF Without Annotations
> 
> 1. **Save As PDF**:
>    - After you have your changes displayed as you want, go to **“File”** > **“Save As”**.
>    - Choose **PDF** from the file format dropdown menu.
>    - This will save a clean copy of your document with all changes integrated without showing the annotation comments.
> 
> ### Alternative: Exporting with Highlighted Changes Only
> 
> 1. If you want to keep the highlighted changes but remove all comments:
>    - First, ensure **all changes are highlighted**.
>    - Then, go to **“Review” > “Restrict Editing”** and click **“Stop Protection”** to remove any restrictions on editing.
>    - Next, select **“File” > “Save As” > “PDF”** and save it.
> 
> Using these options will give you flexibility in how your document appears both in Word and when converted to a PDF. If you have any further questions or need more help, feel free to ask!
